\title{Gravity from a Kater Pendulum: Second-Year Laboratory Report}
\author{J.~Whitfield}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A Kater reversible pendulum was used to determine the local
acceleration due to gravity from five reversal pairs.
The combined result is $g = 9.787 \pm 0.014$~m/s$^2$, where
the uncertainty combines the scatter of the pairs with the
timing and knife-edge contributions.
The value lies 0.2\% below the nominal standard value, within
the combined uncertainty once the buoyancy and finite-amplitude
corrections are included.
\end{abstract}

\section{Apparatus and Method}
The pendulum is a steel bar with two fixed knife-edges and an
adjustable bob, swung in turn from each knife-edge.
For each reversal pair the bob was adjusted until the two
periods agreed to within one millisecond, and the distance
between the knife-edges was then measured with a travelling
microscope.
Periods were timed over fifty swings with a photogate, and
each configuration was measured twice to check repeatability.
Five reversal pairs were completed in a single laboratory
session.

\section{Results}
The equivalent length and the derived value of $g$ for each
pair are listed in Table~\ref{tab:pairs}.
The five pairs give a mean of $9.787$~m/s$^2$ with a combined
standard uncertainty of $0.014$~m/s$^2$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
Pair & $g$ (m/s$^2$) \\
\midrule
1 & 9.792 \\
2 & 9.781 \\
3 & 9.788 \\
4 & 9.785 \\
5 & 9.789 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Derived values of $g$ for the five reversal pairs}
\label{tab:pairs}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}
The scatter of the five pairs is small compared with the
systematic contributions from the knife-edge measurement and
the timing resolution.
The buoyancy correction raises the value by
$0.002$~m/s$^2$ and the finite-amplitude correction by a
further $0.001$~m/s$^2$; both are included in the quoted
result.

\section{Conclusions}
The Kater pendulum gives $g = 9.787 \pm 0.014$~m/s$^2$ for
this laboratory, 0.2\% below the nominal standard value of
9.807~m/s$^2$ and consistent with it within the combined
uncertainty.
The dominant improvements for a future run are a more rigid
support and a direct measurement of the knife-edge separation
with a laser gauge.

\end{document}
